; lautstark.tech - the record set, and the only place it is written down.
;
; This is the source of truth. STRATO's control panel is a copy of it, made by
; hand, and dns/verify.py is what says whether the copy still matches. Change
; this file first and the panel second, never the other way round - a change
; made only in the panel is a change nobody can review and nobody will find.
;
; Apply and verify:  see dns/README.md
;
; SOA and NS are deliberately absent. They belong to whoever is authoritative
; for the zone, which today is STRATO, and writing our own would be writing
; down a guess. verify.py reports the live NS set rather than asserting one.

$ORIGIN lautstark.tech.
$TTL 3600


; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Mail: a real mailbox, at STRATO
; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; This block said "there is none" until 2026-08-28. There is now: the public
; address is steffi@lautstark.tech, and it is what the Impressum and the
; privacy notice of every Lautstark app point at. A null MX next to a
; published contact address is not the stricter setting - it is a contact
; address that silently refuses mail, which for an Impressum is a legal
; problem as much as a practical one.
;
; The panel led the file here rather than the other way round: the mailbox was
; ordered at STRATO and the order set MX and DKIM by itself. Per README, "the
; panel is right, the file drifted -> fix the file and say why". This is why.

; STRATO's inbound host, as the mailbox order set it. Checked live 2026-08-28.
@                   IN  MX     5 smtpin.rzone.de.

; STRATO's outbound ranges, by include rather than by copying addresses that
; are STRATO's to change. "-all" and not "~all": a softfail asks the receiver
; to file the forgery under spam instead of refusing it.
;
; NOT IN THE PANEL YET as of 2026-08-28. The mailbox order added MX and DKIM
; and left the apex TXT empty, so the domain currently sends with no SPF at
; all and DMARC alignment rests on the DKIM signature alone. verify.py reports
; this record as missing, and that report is correct until it is typed in.
@                   IN  TXT    "v=spf1 include:spf.rzone.de -all"

; Reject on failure. This is what the mailbox order published, and it is
; matched here rather than quietly tightened, so that verify.py compares
; against what is actually live.
;
; Worth one deliberate panel edit later: adding "sp=reject; adkim=s; aspf=s"
; extends the rule to every subdomain and demands strict alignment on both.
; Do it after confirming that a real message from the mailbox passes DMARC -
; strict alignment on a domain whose SPF is not published yet is a way to
; reject your own mail.
_dmarc              IN  TXT    "v=DMARC1;p=reject;"

; DKIM is STRATO's to publish and STRATO's to rotate. The live selectors are
;
;   strato-dkim-0002._domainkey   RSA
;   strato-dkim-0003._domainkey   ed25519
;
; and they are deliberately not transcribed as records to verify: a routine
; rotation would then surface here as a failure rather than as normal.
;
; The wildcard "v=DKIM1; p=" that used to sit here revoked every selector, and
; was correct while the domain sent nothing. It is gone. It was not in fact
; breaking the mailbox - a wildcard does not shadow a name that exists, and
; both selectors above are published explicitly - but a revoke-everything
; record next to a signing mailbox is a trap laid for the first rotation that
; lands on a selector nobody published by hand.


; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The website: GitHub Pages
; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THESE ARE NOT READY TO APPLY. Two things have to be true first, and neither
; is true today - see "Before the Pages records" in dns/README.md:
;
;   1. A repository Lautstark/Lautstark.github.io has to exist, be published,
;      and carry lautstark.tech as its custom domain. It does not exist.
;   2. Setting the custom domain on the ORGANISATION site moves every project
;      site with it: the builder becomes lautstark.tech/vorlaut-diy-talker/
;      rather than lautstark.github.io/vorlaut-diy-talker/. That is a decision
;      about published URLs, and the old ones only keep working for as long as
;      GitHub keeps redirecting them.
;
; Apply the mail block above now. Come back to this block when 1 and 2 have
; been decided.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

; GitHub's four documented apex addresses. Checked live against a Pages apex
; on 2026-08-24; they change rarely and verify.py will say when they have.
@                   IN  A      185.199.108.153
@                   IN  A      185.199.109.153
@                   IN  A      185.199.110.153
@                   IN  A      185.199.111.153

@                   IN  AAAA   2606:50c0:8000::153
@                   IN  AAAA   2606:50c0:8001::153
@                   IN  AAAA   2606:50c0:8002::153
@                   IN  AAAA   2606:50c0:8003::153

; www is a CNAME rather than a second set of A records, so that GitHub can
; move its addresses without this file having to know.
www                 IN  CNAME  lautstark.github.io.

; Let's Encrypt only, and no wildcards - which is what GitHub Pages uses to
; issue the certificate for a custom domain. A CAA record does not make
; issuance work; it stops anybody else's from working.
;
; This one has to change if the site ever moves off Pages, and a certificate
; that mysteriously will not issue is what a stale CAA record looks like.
@                   IN  CAA    0 issue "letsencrypt.org"
@                   IN  CAA    0 issuewild ";"
